# Line endings — LF in the repository AND in every working copy, Windows included.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. `bun run lint` is `biome check .`, and Biome formats to LF. With no .gitattributes, a
# Windows checkout under the default core.autocrlf=true writes CRLF to disk, so Biome sees 1,582 files that
# "need to be formatted" — every tracked source file, none of them actually wrong. The gate that CLAUDE.md
# lists first was therefore unusable locally on Windows while passing in CI (Linux, LF), which is the worst
# shape a gate can have: green where nobody reads it, meaningless noise where somebody does. Anyone trying to
# use it either learns to ignore 1,599 errors — and with them the ~17 real findings buried underneath — or
# reformats 2,500 files and collides with everyone else.
#
# Safe to add at any time: `git grep -I --cached -l $'\r'` returns nothing, so no tracked file is stored with
# CRLF and this renormalises nothing. It changes what git WRITES on checkout, not what it stores. Existing
# working copies keep their CRLF until the file is next checked out or `git add --renormalize .` is run.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one Windows-native script, which is invoked by cmd.exe and keeps the endings that shell expects.
deploy/windows/run-service.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touched, never diffed as text. `text=auto` detects most of these on content, but
# saying it outright means a new asset cannot be corrupted by a heuristic that guessed wrong.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary

# Lockfiles are generated, enormous, and reviewed by `bun run lint:lockfile` rather than by eye.
bun.lock linguist-generated=true
